# Git Workflow

How we name branches, write commits, and open PRs. Names and messages describe **what changed**,
not which `docs/workplan.md` item or owner it maps to — the workplan is a planning document, not a
git taxonomy, and its shorthand shouldn't outlive the file it's written in.

## Never use in branch names, PR titles, commit messages, or PR bodies

- Workplan item codes: `s1`, `s6`, `y9`, `u3` (from `docs/workplan.md`)
- Owner names as a namespace: `sagi/...`, `yoav-mission-control`, `person-a`
- Internal workplan terms: `vertical-slice`, `meeting-point-3`, `walking-skeleton`
- Vague buckets: `runtime`, `orchestration`, `foundation` (unless naming a real component)

## Branch names

- **kebab-case**, lowercase, named for the component/feature/fix, not the plan item
- One logical change per branch

| Bad | Good |
|-----|------|
| `s2-mapping-algorithm` | `port-mapping-algorithm-impl` |
| `sagi/plugin-loader` | `dlopen-plugin-registrar` |
| `y9-run-factory` | `simulation-run-factory-mocks` |
| `vertical-slice` | `end-to-end-comparative-run` |

## Starting a new branch

Default to branching from an up-to-date `main` unless told otherwise:

```bash
git checkout main
git pull
git checkout -b <branch-name>
```

## Commit messages

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `<type>: <summary>`.

- **type**: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `ci`, `build`
- **summary**: imperative, lowercase after the colon, ≤ 72 chars
- **body** (optional): what changed and how to verify — no item codes or owner names

| Bad | Good |
|-----|------|
| `S2: port mapping algorithm` | `feat: port MappingAlgorithmImpl to dependency struct ctor` |
| `Yoav: run factory stub` | `feat: add SimulationRunFactoryImpl stub for orchestrator` |
| `docs: update workplan meeting point` | `docs: document per-run output naming in README` |

**Type guide**: `feat` new behavior/capability · `fix` bug fix · `refactor` no behavior change ·
`test` tests only · `docs` README/HLD/AGENTS.md/rules · `chore` tooling/housekeeping · `ci`
CI/CD · `build` CMake/vcpkg/compile settings.

## Commit granularity

Commit like a human implementing a feature — several small commits at natural checkpoints, not one
commit for everything at the end.

- Commit when a coherent slice is done: compiles, a test passes, one sub-behavior is wired
- Typical checkpoint order: scaffolding → core logic → error paths → tests → docs
- Each commit is reviewable on its own — one concern, one message
- Don't batch a whole plan item into one commit, and don't split so finely that a commit doesn't
  build (e.g. `feat: port DroneControlImpl step loop` → `feat: catch movement collision in DroneControl`
  → `test: cover collision containment in DroneControl`)

## PR titles and descriptions

- Title in Conventional Commits style, naming the deliverable: `feat: load hidden map in
  SimulationRunFactoryImpl`, not `Yoav track: run factory`
- **Summary**: what the PR does and why (1–3 bullets)
- **Test plan**: concrete commands or scenarios (e.g. `ctest --preset default`, the exact CLI
  invocation exercised)
- Link to code/components (`MappingAlgorithmImpl`, `error.log`) instead of `docs/workplan.md`
  sections — the plan will keep changing as items land; the PR history shouldn't depend on it
  staying stable to make sense

## Checklist

1. New feature branch? Update `main` from remote, then branch from it.
2. Name the branch from the code change, not a `docs/workplan.md` item code or owner.
3. If the only available name would include an item code or owner name, rename before pushing.
4. Rename existing branches that violate this when you next touch them.
5. Commit incrementally as you implement — don't wait until the item is finished.
